  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Late summer surge of heat and humidity continues with a\nsevere thunderstorm and excessive rainfall threat through \nthis Thursday evening\n\nMain change this forecast cycle was to issue a Flash Flood \nWatch for the entire CWA based on the current wide distribution \nof moderate to heavy rain and embedded/training TSRA along with \na HREF/NAM/MRMS blend of short range model guidance and remote \nsensing.\n\nA seemingly endless train of mid/upper level impulses riding ESE\nalong the southern edge of a persistent jet and within a region\nof broad upper level diffluence and anomalously high PWATs of\nnearly 2 inches will continue to interact with numerous, \nconverging convective outflow boundaries to produce several more\nbouts of heavy showers/TSRA today through at least early\ntonight. \n\nBasin \"Average\" rainfall across the CWA will likely range\nbetween 0.75 to 1.25 inches but as we saw Wed/Wednesday night,\ntraining/and back-building TSRA could easily dump over 3 inches\nof rain in just an hour and lead to significant flash flooding. \n\nThe target areas for the heaviest rainfall today and this\nevening should be the Southwest half of the CWA as a well-\ndefined outflow boundary has pushed into southern PA early\ntoday. We do expect the airmass to recover quickly late this\nmorning/early this afternoon as this first MCS moves SE.\nHowever, a glance just to the left shows another developing MCS\nclose on its heels over West-Central Lake Erie. This feature is\nprogged by high res near term model guidance to track SE twd \nthe laurel and diminish by 14-15Z. By 19-20Z today, more TSRA\nfire across Northern PA and a second larger area\nfollows for this evening over a similar part of the state that\nexperience the heavy rain over the past 24 hours. \n\nAdditionally, increased southwest to westerly flow today will \ncontinue to pump in the late summer heat and very high \ndewpoints. A heat advisory remains in effect from 11a-8p \nThursday across a portion of south central PA where max heat \nindex values are projected to reach/exceed 100F. \n\nAs mentioned above, SPC's severe risk are will encompass \nprimarily the SW half of the state where ML CAPE will be the \nhighest, but the MRGL risk area will cover practically the \nentire rest of Eastern PA. \n\nAn upper level trough passage favors one final round of showers\nand storms on Friday, but fortunately the Labor Day weekend is \ntrending dry. The upper ridge is forecast to retrograde by the \nend of the week which allows for a return to more seasonal \ntemperatures and lower humidity this weekend into early next \nweek.",
